 ## Operating characteristics
-Time and memory complexity are not given in the sources.
+Time and memory complexity are not given in the sources. [G]
 Strengths: Efficiently confines gas to a specific volume without complex boundary checks. The noise offset breaks radial symmetry, making the gas look more natural than a pure cosine falloff [S1, unofficial].
 Limitations: The confinement is spherical/slab-based, not suitable for arbitrary shapes.
 When to use: Simulating steam, smoke, or gas clouds originating from a central point or slab [S1, unofficial].
-When not to use: When the volume shape is not roughly spherical or slab-like, or when complex internal structure requires more than a simple radial falloff.
+When not to use: When the volume shape is not roughly spherical or slab-like, or when complex internal structure requires more than a simple radial falloff. [G]
 
 ## Background
-The method originates from David Ebert's work on procedural modeling of gases and fluids, specifically described in "Procedural Modeling, Animation, and Rendering of Gases, Fluids, and Textures" [S1, unofficial]. It was designed to correct the unnatural appearance of basic turbulence-based gases by confining them to a slab and adding a rising/falloff characteristic. The "steam_slab1" name reflects its specific application to simulating steam rising from a cup [S1, unofficial].
